Agent Response: Thanks — good detail. Below I’ll summarize what’s (likely) deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office for your consulting, and which federal (and a note on MA) forms you’ll need. I’ll also call out a couple of clarifying questions where your answer changes the treatment.

Quick headline recommendations
- It will almost certainly be better to take the standard deduction for 2024 (head of household). Your itemizable items (charity $1,400) are far below the HOH standard deduction, so itemizing won’t help.
- Treat your consulting as self‑employment income: report it on Schedule C, take a home‑office deduction there (actual method will probably give a bigger deduction than the simplified method), pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- The student‑loan interest amount you paid ($4,800) is limited to an above‑the‑line deduction up to $2,500 and is phased out based on MAGI. Given your W‑2 wages ($91,000) + other income, you are likely over the MAGI phaseout for the student loan interest deduction and therefore probably cannot claim it — but we can confirm with exact MAGI rules for tax year 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the old miscellaneous itemized category) are not deductible for most taxpayers through 2025. If the $950 was a business (consulting) expense, you can deduct it on Schedule C — that matters.

Detailed breakdown

1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages: $91,000 — report on Form 1040.
- Consulting: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (self‑employment). Report all 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received and any additional payments even if no 1099 was issued.
- Interest: $1,780 (Chase) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975 — report as interest income on Form 1040. Because total interest > $1,500, you’ll also complete Schedule B.

2) Home office (self‑employment)
- You have a dedicated exclusive home office of 165 sq ft in a 780 sq ft home → business use percentage = 165/780 ≈ 21.15%.
- Annual rent is $1,600 × 12 = $19,200. The home‑office portion of rent (actual method) = $19,200 × 21.1538 ≈ $4,062.
- Two allowed methods:
  a) Actual expenses (recommended here because it gives a larger deduction): prorate rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. (You didn’t list utilities; include them if you pay them and can allocate a business share.)
  b) Simplified: $5 × square feet = 165 × $5 = $825.
- Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method. (If you use the simplified method, you don’t file Form 8829.)
- The home‑office deduction is claimed on Schedule C and reduces your net self‑employment profit. It cannot exceed your net profit from the business for the year (special carryover rules apply for disallowed amounts).

3) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Your Schedule C profit = $12,500 minus business expenses (including home‑office portion and any valid consulting expenses).
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is computed on Schedule SE; you’ll owe both income tax and SE tax on net profit. You can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- If you expect to owe tax because of this consulting income, you should consider making quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) or increasing withholding on your W‑2 paycheck.

4) Student‑loan interest ($4,800)
- The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (above the line), but it phases out based on MAGI. For recent years the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household has started around $75k and completed around $90k — you exceed that range based on your W‑2 alone, so you’re likely ineligible to deduct student loan interest for 2024.
- Whether any or part of your $4,800 can be deducted depends on your exact MAGI and the 2024 phaseout thresholds. If you’d like, I can check the current thresholds and run the numbers with your exact MAGI.

5) Charitable gift ($1,400)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Since your itemized deductions appear to be only $1,400 (plus any state/local taxes you paid), that’s far below the HOH standard deduction, so the charitable gift will likely not produce tax savings if you take the standard deduction.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these $950 were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible (suspended under current law).
- If they were business expenses for your consulting work (supplies, travel, advertising, software, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your net profit. Clarify whether these $950 are business expenses or employee unreimbursed expenses.

7) Itemize vs. standard
- Standard deduction for Head of Household (tax year 2024) is substantially larger than the $1,400 of charitable contributions you reported. Unless you have significant other itemizable amounts (state/local tax withheld or paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), you should take the standard deduction.
- If you want I can run a quick compare if you provide any state/local tax paid and any mortgage interest or large medical expenses for 2024.

8) Forms you will typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule B — if interest > $1,500 (you are over that).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (report consulting income and business expenses).
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method to claim business use of your home.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (half SE tax deduction, student loan interest if eligible).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely here).
- Form 1040‑ES — for making estimated tax payments (if needed).
- Massachusetts: Form 1 (resident return) — you’ll file a MA state return. MA tax rules differ; report your federal AGI and follow MA instructions. (If you want, I can list MA specifics once you confirm you want state guidance.)

Records to keep
- Receipts and logs for the home office (floor plan, square footage calculation), rent receipts, utilities, tenant/homeowner insurance.
- Records for consulting income (1099s, invoices, bank deposits) and all business expenses.
- Receipts for charitable donation (your $1,400 receipt).
- Student loan statements showing interest paid (Form 1098‑E if issued).
- Bank statements and 1099‑INT forms for interest.

Next steps / clarifying questions
1. Were the $950 unreimbursed expenses for your consulting business or for your W‑2 job? If they were business expenses, they belong on Schedule C; if for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible.
2. Did any of your 6 consulting clients issue 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC? (You should report the full amount regardless of 1099s.)
3. Do you want me to run a quick numeric example showing the effect of:
   - claiming the home‑office actual expense vs simplified method,
   - calculating estimated SE tax,
   - and confirming whether any student loan interest remains deductible given 2024 MAGI phaseout thresholds?
4. Do you want state (MA) filing guidance and whether MA allows any of the above differently?

If you want, I can run the numbers with the assumptions above (use actual home‑office expense, treat $950 as business expenses or not, and use the common MAGI thresholds) and give a rough estimate of taxable income and self‑employment tax.
