Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll likely need. Quick note: I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. If you want a definitive answer (especially about the student‑loan phase‑out and state rules), consider a tax pro.

Quick summary of your numbers (from your message)
- W‑2 wages: $91,000
- Consulting (self‑employment) income: $12,500
- Interest: Chase $1,780 + Fidelity $195 = $1,975
- Rent: $1,600/month → $19,200/year (780 sq ft total)
- Home office: 165 sq ft exclusive (165 / 780 ≈ 21.15% of home)
- Student loan interest reported: $4,800
- Charitable cash: $1,400 (receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $950 — need to know whether these are employee (W‑2) expenses or business expenses for consulting

What is deductible (high‑level)
- Consulting business expenses (if you are self‑employed for that work): deductible on Schedule C. That includes:
  - Home office deduction (see options below) for the business portion of your home
  - Business supplies, mileage, phone/Internet portion, and any of the $950 that are business expenses
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net consulting income; half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line deduction).
- Interest income: reportable on Form 1040; because your interest total is $1,975 (> $1,500), you’ll likely need Schedule B.
- Charitable gifts: deductible only if you itemize. You have $1,400 in cash gifts with receipt (keep the receipt).
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500 per year (adjusted depending on MAGI and phaseouts). You cannot deduct more than the annual limit, and eligibility phases out at higher MAGI — see IRS rules or provide MAGI for a more precise check.
- Unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses: currently disallowed for most employees (suspended for tax years 2018–2025 under federal law). If the $950 are employee job expenses for your W‑2 job, you generally cannot deduct them on your federal return. If they are business expenses for your consulting, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Home office — two methods (you must be self‑employed or an independent contractor to claim)
1) Simplified method
   - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
   - Your deduction = 165 sq ft × $5 = $825.
   - Simpler recordkeeping; no Form 8829 needed.
2) Actual‑expense method
   - You allocate a business percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs attributable to the space, etc.). You said rent is $19,200/year; business portion = 21.1538% × $19,200 ≈ $4,061.54. Add any portion of utilities or other eligible expenses for the space.
   - For homeowners, depreciation can also be part of the calculation; as a renter you generally do NOT take depreciation on the landlord’s property, but you can allocate rent, utilities, etc.
   - You must use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C if you use the actual method.
Which method to choose?
- Based purely on rent alone, actual method (just rent portion) here gives ~ $4,062 vs $825 simplified — actual is larger. But actual requires accurate records of rent/utilities/etc. If you have other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance) that increase the business share, actual is almost certainly better.
- Use the method that gives the larger allowable deduction after considering recordkeeping and any other office‑related expenses.

Rough tax/AGI effects (illustrative)
- Total gross income ≈ $105,475.
- If you use actual home office and treat the $950 as business expenses:
  - Net consulting profit ≈ 12,500 − (home office ≈ 4,062) − 950 = ≈ $7,488.
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × $7,488 ≈ $1,058 (approx). Half of that (~$529) is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 and phases out by MAGI — from the rough AGI above you may be above the phase‑out range, so you might not get the full $2,500 (or any). I can check more precisely if you want me to estimate MAGI after adjustments.
- Charitable $1,400 will only help if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- You should compare the total of your itemizable deductions (charitable gifts, state & local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, mortgage interest, medical beyond threshold, etc.) to the standard deduction for head of household.
- With the info supplied (only $1,400 charitable and maybe student loan interest up to $2,500 if eligible), your itemized total looks small (< $5,000) and is very likely less than the standard deduction for head of household. So most likely the standard deduction will be better.
- Exception: if you have sizable state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other deductible items you didn’t list, itemizing could become worthwhile.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income — for the deductible half of SE tax, certain student loan interest entries)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual home‑office method; not needed for simplified method
- Form 1099‑NEC(s) or Form 1099‑MISC: you should receive 1099s from clients who paid you $600+; even if you don’t receive them, report the income
- Form 1099‑INT (from Chase and Fidelity) — interest reporting; Schedule B if required

Other practical items and recommendations
- Clarify the $950: are those business expenses for consulting or unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 employer? If they’re business expenses for the consulting, they belong on Schedule C; if they’re W‑2 employee expenses, they're likely not deductible federally.
- Clarify the $4,800 student loan interest: is that interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for, or an aggregate from multiple years? The deduction is for interest paid in the tax year filed and is limited to $2,500 (and phases out by MAGI).
- Keep receipts: charitable receipts, documentation for business expenses, proof of home‑office measurement and exclusive use, bank/broker 1099s, and FedLoan statement for student loan interest.
- Estimated taxes: with self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Consider retirement contributions (SEP IRA or Solo 401(k)) — they can reduce taxable income if you want to lower AGI/taxes owed.
- State taxes: you’re in MA — confirm Massachusetts rules for itemizing, student loan treatment, and home‑office treatment (state rules can differ).

Next steps I can take for you
- I can estimate your AGI and a rough federal tax owed or refund using the numbers you gave (I’ll need to assume whether the $950 is business expenses and whether the $4,800 interest is for the tax year).
- I can prepare a checklist of receipts/documents to gather for filing.
- Per your saved preference, I can also send this summary to vince2000@gmail.com. Would you like me to send it now, and do you want me to include any additional notes or documents?

